    "blurb": "Nakhchivan city, Nakhchivan, Autonomous Region of Azerbaijan: Population - 95K Two words: Holy fucking shit. Okay, that's three. Coming from Baku to Nakhchivan (city proper) is like witnessing two completely incongruous siblings living under the same roof. Despite people's surprise, the Nakhchivan Autonomous…",
    "content": "Nakhchivan city, Nakhchivan, Autonomous Region of Azerbaijan: Population - 95K\n\nTwo words: Holy fucking shit. Okay, that's three. Coming from Baku to Nakhchivan (city proper) is like witnessing two completely incongruous siblings living under the same roof. Despite people's surprise, the Nakhchivan Autonomous Republic of Azerbaijan, an exclave sandwiched snuggly between Armenia and Iran, is not difficult at all to visit (though the tourist infrastructure says otherwise). Interestingly, Nakhchivan is only a short 40-minute drive to the Iranian border and an hour long flight from Baku. Azerbaijan Airlines offers like 6 to 8 daily flights from Heydar Aliyev's domestic terminal to Nakhchivan Airport. So, that's how I got here.\n\nUpon my landing, I was immediately apprehended by the police demanding that I delete the picture of the Nakhchivan Airport sign that I took. I did delete the photo but you can still find it below, suckers. Afterwards, I felt the police presence everywhere, as patrols would endlessly surveil the city, making the environment feel fairly tense for a visitor like me. I got plenty of looks from the locals, as I assume who were trying to figure how I fit in their dialogue as a foreigner. Speaking of locals, the people dress much differently in Nakhchivan than they do to their Baku counterparts, as the demographic leans toward the older side and are often found wearing more conservative garbs. \n\nOne of the first things you'll notice besides the police presence is the dramatic overview of the mountains that cradle the region. In the city, you can find the Haydar Mosque, a marvelous cerulean structure that serves as a ceremonial monument and offers a bold reminder to the Islamic presence in Azerbaijan. The city is much less developed compared to Baku, yet due to the wide roads that fit the setting, an emptiness is caressed in the cityscape that makes solo traveling an awkward and intimidating endeavor.",
    "reactions": [
      {
        "e": "🤌",
        "c": 6
      }
    ],
    "comments": [
      {
        "by": "duck",
        "date": "2025-03-29",
        "text": "Going about can be challenging, as cellular connection is extremely spotty and the number of Russian and English speakers are very low. You can come across the Momine Khatun Mausoleum in Naxçıvan Mədəniyyət Park, which ever leans-so-slightly. \n\nThere is not much to see in the city but the reason I came to Nakhchivan was to explore the outer sights. After a 20-minute drive from the center, I climbed 5,000 steps upward to see the view from Əlincə qalası or Alinja Tower. With an incredible view all to myself, I genuinely grasped the reality that very few people have experienced by visiting Nakhchivan and climbing to the fortress. An actual-bucket list goal was marked off with this visit. Near the vicinity of Alinja is Ashabi-Kahf, a historic sight where a small mosque lays beneath a towering canyon and an adjacent cave ladled with storied myths and tradition. I knew nearly nothing of this feature but the view amidst the cliffsides of Ashabi-Kahf rivaled the views atop Alinja Tower.\n\nSincerely, Nakhchivan is breathtaking due to the sweeping mountainside which serve the backdrop to the city. Moreover, the city and region offers an experience drastically different to that of Baku. I do not think I would ever brave to return to this exclave but Nakchivan certainly will be an experience I will remember. From my own take, the region does not seem conducive to success. I asked my tour guide who spoke English about what they do in Nakhchivan. Her answer? \"Farming and taxi drivers. If the parents could save enough money, they send their children to Baku.\" Maybe with better days ahead, this quite overlooked region of the world can garner the exposure it deserves, and hopefully the people belonging there find themselves with a more fulfilling future. Currently, much of Nakhchivan's existence solely relies on Turkey as life support as it offers a geopolitical advantage by acting as a buffer state between Turkey, Azerbaijan and neighboring Iran.",
        "reactions": []
      },
      {
        "by": "duck",
        "date": "2025-03-29",
        "text": "deepseek ahh",
        "reactions": []
      }
    ]
